1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,383
Water held high behind a dam.

2
00:00:02,383 --> 00:00:06,053
Let it rush down through a
turbine, and the turbine spins.

3
00:00:06,053 --> 00:00:08,991
The spinning makes
electricity for a whole town.

4
00:00:09,391 --> 00:00:11,350
Your cells have tiny dams too.

5
00:00:11,350 --> 00:00:15,269
And you breathe in oxygen every
few seconds to keep them running.

6
00:00:15,719 --> 00:00:18,668
Aarav thought about this
while panting on his bicycle.

7
00:00:18,668 --> 00:00:21,752
He asked: where does the
oxygen I breathe actually go?

8
00:00:21,752 --> 00:00:23,408
And how do NADH become ATP?

9
00:00:23,758 --> 00:00:25,251
Here is the short answer.

10
00:00:25,251 --> 00:00:28,362
Electrons from NADH pass down
a chain in the mitochondria.

11
00:00:28,362 --> 00:00:32,161
Oxygen catches them at the very end,
and a spinning enzyme makes ATP.

12
00:00:32,661 --> 00:00:36,499
By the end, you will know the electron
transport system, its five complexes,

13
00:00:36,499 --> 00:00:39,630
ATP synthase, and how many ATP
each NADH and FADH two make.

14
00:00:40,280 --> 00:00:41,831
First, what are NADH
and FADH two carrying?

15
00:00:42,181 --> 00:00:46,228
Glycolysis and the Krebs cycle took
hydrogen and electrons from glucose.

16
00:00:46,228 --> 00:00:48,032
NADH and FADH two hold them.

17
00:00:48,032 --> 00:00:49,602
They are full of energy.

18
00:00:50,052 --> 00:00:53,005
Now they give these electrons
to a chain of carriers.

19
00:00:53,005 --> 00:00:55,949
This chain is the electron
transport system, or ETS.

20
00:00:55,949 --> 00:00:58,767
It lies in the inner membrane
of the mitochondrion.

21
00:00:59,417 --> 00:01:01,024
Let's zoom in to find it.

22
00:01:01,374 --> 00:01:04,602
Here is a cell, with
mitochondria in its cytoplasm.

23
00:01:05,102 --> 00:01:06,946
Zoom into one mitochondrion.

24
00:01:06,946 --> 00:01:08,449
It has two membranes.

25
00:01:08,449 --> 00:01:10,654
The inner one folds into cristae.

26
00:01:11,154 --> 00:01:12,831
Zoom into the inner membrane.

27
00:01:12,831 --> 00:01:15,163
Protein complexes sit in a row inside it.

28
00:01:15,163 --> 00:01:18,996
The space between the two membranes
is the intermembrane space, and inside

29
00:01:18,996 --> 00:01:19,944
is the matrix.

30
00:01:20,594 --> 00:01:23,606
Now follow the electrons
down the chain, step by step.

31
00:01:23,956 --> 00:01:24,856
Step one.

32
00:01:24,653 --> 00:01:27,961
NADH gives its electrons to
complex one, NADH dehydrogenase.

33
00:01:27,961 --> 00:01:30,581
They pass to a small
carrier called ubiquinone.

34
00:01:31,331 --> 00:01:32,231
Step two.

35
00:01:32,019 --> 00:01:35,719
FADH two gives its electrons to
ubiquinone too, through complex two.

36
00:01:36,469 --> 00:01:37,369
Step three.

37
00:01:37,284 --> 00:01:41,139
Reduced ubiquinone passes the electrons
to cytochrome c, through complex

38
00:01:41,139 --> 00:01:43,505
three, the cytochrome b c one complex.

39
00:01:44,255 --> 00:01:45,155
Step four.

40
00:01:45,059 --> 00:01:49,206
Cytochrome c is a small mobile protein
on the outer surface of the inner

41
00:01:49,206 --> 00:01:50,106
membrane.

42
00:01:49,908 --> 00:01:53,455
It carries electrons to complex
four, cytochrome c oxidase.

43
00:01:53,455 --> 00:01:57,538
This complex has cytochromes a and
a three, and two copper centres.

44
00:01:58,288 --> 00:01:59,188
Step five.

45
00:01:59,130 --> 00:02:03,599
At the end, oxygen takes the electrons
and hydrogen, and forms water.

46
00:02:03,599 --> 00:02:06,028
Oxygen is the final hydrogen acceptor.

47
00:02:06,778 --> 00:02:07,825
Remember this.

48
00:02:07,825 --> 00:02:10,349
Oxygen comes in only at the very end.

49
00:02:10,949 --> 00:02:12,635
So where is the ATP made?

50
00:02:12,635 --> 00:02:14,583
In complex five, ATP synthase.

51
00:02:14,933 --> 00:02:18,602
As electrons pass down the chain,
their energy pushes protons, which are

52
00:02:18,602 --> 00:02:20,939
hydrogen ions, into the
intermembrane space.

53
00:02:20,939 --> 00:02:23,749
The protons pile up there,
like water behind a dam.

54
00:02:24,199 --> 00:02:28,358
They flow back into the matrix through
F zero, a channel in the membrane.

55
00:02:28,358 --> 00:02:32,655
F one, the head piece, uses this flow
to make ATP from ADP and phosphate.

56
00:02:32,655 --> 00:02:34,993
For each ATP, two protons pass through.

57
00:02:35,643 --> 00:02:39,179
This way of making ATP is called
oxidative phosphorylation.

58
00:02:39,529 --> 00:02:42,793
In photosynthesis, light energy
builds the proton gradient.

59
00:02:42,793 --> 00:02:44,416
That is photophosphorylation.

60
00:02:44,866 --> 00:02:49,785
In respiration, the energy of oxidation
and reduction builds the gradient.

61
00:02:49,785 --> 00:02:52,073
That is oxidative phosphorylation.

62
00:02:52,073 --> 00:02:53,733
Both use ATP synthase.

63
00:02:54,383 --> 00:02:56,578
Now, how many ATP does each carrier give?

64
00:02:56,928 --> 00:02:58,259
Each NADH gives three ATP.

65
00:02:58,709 --> 00:03:00,369
Each FADH two gives two ATP.

66
00:03:00,369 --> 00:03:03,590
Its electrons skip complex one
and enter through complex two.

67
00:03:04,190 --> 00:03:05,090
Your turn.

68
00:03:04,922 --> 00:03:07,697
Which gives more ATP,
one NADH or one FADH two?

69
00:03:07,697 --> 00:03:08,773
Pause and decide.

70
00:03:11,973 --> 00:03:12,873
One NADH!

71
00:03:12,615 --> 00:03:13,742
It gives three ATP.

72
00:03:13,742 --> 00:03:15,314
One FADH two gives only two.

73
00:03:15,964 --> 00:03:17,416
Now, a worked example.

74
00:03:17,416 --> 00:03:21,186
One glucose sends eight NADH and two
FADH two from the matrix into the

75
00:03:21,186 --> 00:03:22,086
chain.

76
00:03:21,710 --> 00:03:22,637
How many ATP?

77
00:03:22,987 --> 00:03:24,982
Eight NADH times three is twenty four ATP.

78
00:03:25,432 --> 00:03:27,202
Two FADH two times two is four ATP.

79
00:03:27,652 --> 00:03:31,834
Twenty four plus four is twenty eight
ATP, from the matrix carriers alone.

80
00:03:31,834 --> 00:03:34,864
The full count for glucose
comes in the next episode.

81
00:03:35,514 --> 00:03:36,976
Now, the exam trap.

82
00:03:37,326 --> 00:03:40,536
Many students think oxygen
is used in the Krebs cycle.

83
00:03:40,936 --> 00:03:45,505
But oxygen works only at the end of
the electron transport system, as the

84
00:03:45,505 --> 00:03:47,110
final hydrogen acceptor.

85
00:03:47,110 --> 00:03:49,838
Still, without it the whole chain stops.

86
00:03:50,488 --> 00:03:51,524
Where is this asked?

87
00:03:51,524 --> 00:03:54,729
As statement questions on the complexes,
cytochrome c, ATP synthase, and

88
00:03:54,729 --> 00:03:56,273
the ATP from NADH and FADH two.

89
00:03:56,873 --> 00:03:58,023
Exam level now.

90
00:03:58,023 --> 00:03:59,719
Read the four statements.

91
00:03:59,719 --> 00:04:00,918
Pause and solve.

92
00:04:06,318 --> 00:04:07,552
The answer is B.

93
00:04:07,552 --> 00:04:10,250
Statements one, two and four are correct.

94
00:04:10,750 --> 00:04:14,011
Complex four has cytochromes a and
a three and two copper centres.

95
00:04:14,011 --> 00:04:16,714
Ubiquinone takes electrons
from both NADH and FADH two.

96
00:04:16,714 --> 00:04:19,234
And two protons pass
through F zero for each ATP.

97
00:04:19,734 --> 00:04:21,253
Statement three is wrong.

98
00:04:21,253 --> 00:04:23,548
F zero is the channel in the membrane.

99
00:04:23,548 --> 00:04:25,940
F one is the head piece that makes ATP.

100
00:04:26,540 --> 00:04:28,081
Here is your recap.

101
00:04:28,081 --> 00:04:29,665
Screenshot this card.

102
00:04:32,865 --> 00:04:35,050
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

103
00:04:35,450 --> 00:04:36,350
One.

104
00:04:35,832 --> 00:04:37,754
Where in the mitochondrion is the ETS?

105
00:04:38,654 --> 00:04:39,554
Two.

106
00:04:39,103 --> 00:04:41,225
What is the final hydrogen acceptor?

107
00:04:42,125 --> 00:04:43,025
Three.

108
00:04:42,546 --> 00:04:44,115
How many ATP does one NADH give?

109
00:04:45,215 --> 00:04:49,488
Next time: how many ATP does one glucose
really make, and why is respiration

110
00:04:49,488 --> 00:04:50,597
called amphibolic?

111
00:04:50,597 --> 00:04:53,201
We find out in the
respiratory balance sheet.
